{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310713,"nonce":"pSusd2E1vNM/d7cXI4npC3gYmbg=","title":"FIXED 66506ae9 — Std.string never boxed its arg, so String printed \u003cunknown type N\u003e and a type-erased generic T SIGSEGV'd; fix = route ...","message":"Found 2026-07-28 chasing the `test_stringbuf_stringtools` regression\n(`\u003cunknown type 315867136\u003e` where `Hello World` belonged).\n\n`Std.string` mapped straight to `haxe_std_string_ptr`, which reads its arg as\na `DynamicValue` and dispatches on `type_id`. **Nothing boxed the argument.**\nInt/Float/Bool happened to get boxed by `maybe_box_for_extern_call`; String sat\nin that function's pass-through arm, so a raw `HaxeString*` arrived and its data\npointer was read as a type_id.\n\n- `Std.string(\"Hello\")` → `\u003cunknown type 280133672\u003e` — for the ONE type where\n  it should be the identity.\n- `Std.string(x:T)` in a type-erased generic → `cast i64 → *void` then deref =\n  **SIGSEGV**. Broken for every T, not just String.\n\n**The arg cannot be boxed at the coercion site.** I tried; it segfaults. The\nmapping declares `PtrVoid`, and that same descriptor is used by *raw-HaxeString*\ncallees — `haxe_string_println(s: *const HaxeString)` vs\n`haxe_std_string_ptr(dynamic_ptr: *mut u8)`. `PtrVoid` is overloaded, so no rule\non the expected `IrType` can decide boxing.\n\n**FIX (66506ae9):** an arm in `try_lower_special_runtime_call` for\n`haxe_std_string_ptr` routing through **`convert_to_string_with_hint`** — String\nis the identity, a type parameter dispatches on its fixed-up tag via\n`haxe_value_to_string_by_tag`, Int/Float/Bool use their to-string wrappers, and\nonly a genuinely Dynamic value reaches `haxe_std_string_ptr`. ValueType enums\nkeep their own routing (return `None` so the call site handles them).\n\nWrong: (1) add `haxe_box_string_ptr` to the runtime — E0428, it already exists;\n(2) add a String arm to `box_primitive_to_dynamic` — inert, wrong function;\n(3) box String in `maybe_box_for_extern_call` — **SIGSEGV**, killed println.\n\nWhat localised it in one step: **`rayzor dump \u003cfile\u003e -O 0 --function main`**.\nInt showed `call box_int` then `call fn5`; String showed `call fn5` with no box\nat all. Read `-O0`; `-O2` misleads. `debug!` is dead code here — there is NO\nlogger init in `src/main.rs`, so `RUST_LOG` does nothing; use `eprintln!` gated\non an env var. See [[feedback_verify_codegen_by_disasm]].\n\n`g\u003cT\u003e(1.5)` inlined → tag stayed 0 → `haxe_value_to_string_by_tag` printed the\nf64 bits (`4609434218613702656`); same via `\"\" + x`. `ir_type_to_type_tag` was\ninnocent (F64→4). **The call bitcasts its arg to the callee's erased I64 param,\nso the caller's REGISTER TYPE reads back I64** and the inliner's inference\nrejects I64 as \"still erased\" → empty `type_sub_map`. Fix = `concrete_arg_type`\nlooks through the defining Cast/BitCast. Non-inlined `StringBuf.add(1.5)` was\nalready correct (its arg register kept F64) — that asymmetry is the tell.\n\nSame commit: Cranelift had **no bitcast arm for narrow ints**, so Bool erased as\nI8→I64 installed a trap stub (`W0020 Unsupported bitcast from types::I8 to\ntypes::I64`) for ANY function passing a Bool to a generic. Added general\nint-width arms; **uextend, not sextend** — `true` is 0xFF and sign-extending it\nreads back as -1.\n\n**ROOT (finally measured, 2026-07-28): the receiver's TypeId resolves to an\n`Array` entry in the CONSUMING module.** The probe that settles it prints the\nreceiver TypeKind at the `get_stdlib_runtime_info` hit in `lower_field_access`:\n\n    [FIELD] field=length recv=Some(Array { element_type: TypeId(2) })\n            -\u003e class_match=Array runtime=array_length\n\nSo the lookup correctly asks for `Array.length` — it is a RIGHT answer to the\nWRONG question. `sb.push(1)` also raises no type error, confirming the TAST\nreally believes it is an Array. This is cross-module **TypeId drift**\n([[bugs_xmodule_resolution_disease_cluster]]), not a dispatch bug, and not\n\"receiver is Dynamic\" as I first wrote — that was a DIFFERENT set of `length`\nreads in the same run. Fixing it means the class TypeId surviving into the\nconsuming module; `resolve_builtin_type` returns None for \"StringBuf\", so the\ndrift is in type-table numbering, not name resolution.\n\nWhy the old StringBuf looked correct: field #1 was `b:String`, so reading it and\ntaking a length was accidentally right. The `rayzor.Bytes` rewrite (9a67f9f0)\nmade field #1 a buffer and exposed it. `trace(buf.length)` is still correct —\nonly the string-concat context mistypes.\n\n- Gating `try_dynamic_stdlib_property_dispatch` on an erased receiver — that\n  helper is NOT the emitter here; `get_stdlib_runtime_info` is.\n- A name-global \"a class declares this getter\" guard — breaks the legitimate\n  `b.length` on `rayzor.Bytes`, which is erased identically.\n- Setting a class hint at `New` in `lower_expression` — the hint lands on the\n  alloc register, but the field access uses a CAST of it, and the failing reads\n  happen where the hint map is empty anyway.\n- `field_class_names` has no owner for the use-site field; `qualified_name` on\n  the field symbol is None. No owner discriminator exists at that point.\n\n- `; fnN = @name` comments in `rayzor dump` are only valid WITHIN the dump that\n  produced them; resolving an id against a different dump gives a wrong name\n  (I \"confirmed\" `haxe_array_get_f64` for a 1-arg call this way).\n- The mapping's `runtime_name` is the WRAPPER (`array_length`); the dump shows\n  the LINKED name (`haxe_array_length`, renamed in `wasm_linker.rs`). Probing\n  `get_or_register_extern_function` for the linked name never fires.\n- Release backtraces collapse to `HirToMirContext` with no method names —\n  `std::backtrace::Backtrace::force_capture()` is useless for locating a site\n  here. Probe the CONSUMER and print what it matched instead.\n\n`find_instance_property_by_name` returned the FIRST zero-arg property with a\nmatching name; `length` alone has TEN distinct targets. Now accepted only when\nevery match names the same runtime function. Suite 167/167 with a\nbyte-identical output diff — nothing was relying on the arbitrary pick.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_std_string_dynamic_unbox_and_erased_generics` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}